{"id": "striviaqa0146", "pool": "striviaqa", "tier": "conservative", "audio_s": 3.4, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.5192, "eot_read_ms": 26, "reference_answer": "noah (accepted aliases: nooh, nuach, flood bible, נוֹחַ, noah hebrew bible, no, noahtic, noahian, no ah, noah and ark, noah)", "query": "Who was the first person in the bible to get drunk?", "transcript": "Who was the first person in the Bible to get drunk?", "mode": "local", "answer": "Well, actually, nobody in the Bible got drunk as a result of being the first person. The story you might be thinking of is about Noah and the great flood. After God instructed Noah to build an ark because the earth was filled with violence, God sent rain for forty days and forty nights, causing a flood that destroyed all life except for Noah, his family, and the animals on the ark. After the waters receded, Noah released a dove, a raven, and then a dove again to see if the ground was dry. When the dove didn't return, Noah knew it was safe to leave the ark. Then he built an altar and offered sacrifices to God, and God made a covenant with him, promising never again to destroy the earth with a flood. While wine wasn't specifically mentioned in the account of the flood itself, after the flood, during the story of the Tower of Babel, there is a reference to Noah planting a vineyard and drinking its wine, which made him drunk. But he wasn't the first person ever; that distinction belongs to Adam and Eve.", "answer_ms": 6640}
